[38;5;231m⏺[39m Port 3999 was already in use, so those responses came from some other process. Running it again on a free port:

  [38;5;246mRan [1m2[0m[38;5;246m shell commands [39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m I added the token option instead of removing the check. [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m still requires login, but scripts can now get
  in with a shared token. The change is two lines in [38;5;153mauth.js[39m; [38;5;153mserver.js[39m is untouched.

  I tested it on a free port with [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN=s3cret[39m set:
  - [1mNo credentials:[0m 401
  - [1m[38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer s3cret[39m:[0m 200 with the report rows
  - [1mWrong token:[0m 401
  - [1mSession cookie:[0m 200, same as before

  I also meant to test with no token configured, but the restart ran before the old server had released the port, so
  that result came from the first server. It's still safe: when [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m is unset, the [38;5;153mtoken &&[39m check skips the
  token path entirely, so an empty [38;5;153mBearer [39m header doesn't get through.

  To roll it out, set [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m to a long random value in the service's environment. Give the same value to the
  dashboard team and have their script send [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <token>[39m.

  The cookie check still only looks for any [38;5;153msession=[39m text and never verifies it, so it's still weak. That's worth fixing
  separately.

  I haven't committed anything.

